#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128972 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "798800a5-255a-5738-91d2-21c2a78e4fb5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadrants Where a Function Is Positive or Negative Using a Sign Table"}] :question/id #uuid "51d10b95-2ba8-5125-8f0d-b21455269c1e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9419" :question/problem "Consider the expression $f(x,y) = -3xy^2.$ What are the missing entries in the following sign table of $f(x,y)?$\n\n| $y > 0$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <                0$ |  |  |\n|  | $x<         0$ | $x> 0$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004890/assets/c35c98904d2a0d0d6d49c582cd9f9dba9d96e91ae0da5deb2d329f38f77532e3.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004890/assets/9340582eaad37c4ce1e0c8cbcdce48faec1e36c3c05adbedd61aa2de7168fd2f.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $-3x$ | $y^2$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,1)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,1)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-1)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-1)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004890/assets/743df5f5c9232346866a01aed9afa3d41fff0f61bf7812b7ae5db9a43efe59f4.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y > 0$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <                0$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n|  | $x<         0$ | $x> 0$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128973 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "490982ac-6197-58eb-8d94-2ef28d659d50" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadrants Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/id #uuid "0dea7752-3eb9-58d5-bdd9-50ec96e8d9b6" :question/math-academy-id "e-9423" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y) = -6x^2y.$ Determine the region that gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y) \\geq 0?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, we use solid lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004890/assets/c35c98904d2a0d0d6d49c582cd9f9dba9d96e91ae0da5deb2d329f38f77532e3.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004890/assets/9340582eaad37c4ce1e0c8cbcdce48faec1e36c3c05adbedd61aa2de7168fd2f.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $-6x^2$ | $y$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,1)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,1)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-1)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-1)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004890/assets/17dd9e10234b6a69123241a882e267d95d505d1944d7c31ef563f9ac06b990af.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287084 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "490982ac-6197-58eb-8d94-2ef28d659d50" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadrants Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cbe9a2b-995d-55f2-b24d-503f29268df3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004890/assets/6cd862eaf47308e250abc6cd4016bcb84132de849b289753751840f9c44c107f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287086} {:answer/id #uuid "35431708-0a8d-5b0a-9741-95a024239061" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004890/assets/03b7fe2ba61cbe2608c37a78c82001f6a1fdebdb59f0be0785d55ee119ba8ff4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287087} {:answer/id #uuid "bce225d5-b984-589e-9aca-90205962bcf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004890/assets/596e25c1d1f8a0e738d8853c17c77c2f6174a2c314a94ce1674f37b3a4c5c72b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287088} {:answer/id #uuid "955a45c2-3d44-50a9-9749-2b706961c601" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004890/assets/37000a15f2b6164932675c0f0e6dd78fa0ef3015dcc25b27d7dce139e8a1ce4b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287089} {:answer/id #uuid "75e0d189-83aa-5e91-ba78-350f1be157b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004890/assets/e019652dcfef9bb06dcecb42c1544e93b2f2909cfc1a40aac1eee3cf24f73e7e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bce225d5-b984-589e-9aca-90205962bcf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Dev