tion-capture/14004890/assets/8fde51124507af53cd4b653856f12976a675dc4bf10a274c84623797ad8e1f2c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287102} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a91e66f-c73d-57e6-a225-7c2a8f32abf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "53a61ee0-cc4e-5171-b4ee-13011d608e96" :question/math-academy-id "q-121262" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y)=-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y)\\le 0?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, we use solid lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004890/assets/c44c38febc908fe5e941978bf44bab2e2cb4bd250765950d84f9294c9c4c9c4b.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004890/assets/209576cd78e9beb1c0f7a9bce8267163df1668a830ffc5c06918f8a726600fbe.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $-{x}^{2}$ | ${y}^{2}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(-1,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)\\le 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004890/assets/fc2a67dd63a482a37cf91c66c1b9fde01ea8c26ba0e3d6f7b8a62c112aae4bb0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287112 :knowl